 _read_uint(coder):\n    n = 0\n    shift = 0\n    while True:\n        b = coder.read(1)[0]\n        n = n | ((b & 0x7f) << shift)\n        if not b & 0x80:\n            break\n        shift += 7\n    return n\n```\n\nMy encoder (little-endian 7-bit groups with continuation bit) matches this. If instead it's big-endian style (`n = (n << 7) | (b & 0x7f)`), my encoder would be wrong. get_info will tell. The ABC format uses little-endian 7-bit groups, and youtube_dl's implementation... let me recall the actual swfinterp.py:\n\n```python\ndef _read_byte(coder):\n    return coder.read(1)[0]\n\n\ndef _read_uint(coder):\n    n = 0\n    shift = 0\n    while True:\n        b = coder.read(1)[0]\n        n |= (b & 0x7f) << shift\n        if not b & 0x80:\n            break\n        shift += 7\n    return n\n\n\ndef _s24(coder):\n    bs = coder.read(3)\n    if bs[2] & 0x80:\n        v = -((bs[0] | (bs[1] << 8) | ((bs[2] & 0x7f) << 16)) ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me just query.\n\nActually, I recall from youtube_dl swfinterp.py:\n\n```python\ndef _read_byte(coder):\n    return coder.read(1)[0]\n\n\ndef _read_uint(coder):\n    n = 0\n    for i in range(5):\n        b = coder.read(1)[0]\n        n = (n << 7) | (b & 0x7f)\n        if not b & 0x80:\n            break\n    return n\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me actually think about the real source. From youtube-dl master swfinterp.py:\n\n```python\ndef _read_byte(coder):\n    return coder.read(1)[0]\n\n\ndef _read_uint(coder):\n    n = 0\n    shift = 0\n    while True:\n        b = _read_byte(coder)\n        n |= (b & 0x7f) << shift\n        if not (b & 0x80):\n            break\n        shift += 7\n    return n\n\n\ndef _s24(coder):\n    b = coder.read(3)\n    v = b[0] | (b[1] << 8) | (b[2] << 16)\n    if v & 0x800000:\n        v -= 0x1000000\n    return v\n\n\ndef _u30(coder):\n    v = _read_uint(coder)\n    assert v & 0xf0000000 == 0\n    return v\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident it's little-endian 7-bit groups (standard