 setlocal_3 (215), getlocal_3 (211), returnvalue -> 3. Covers 813-828.\n\n  Actually simpler: one test per opcode or combined. Let me combine: code = pushbyte 1, setlocal_1, getlocal_1, returnvalue. Another: getlocal_0, returnvalue -> variables. Another: pushbyte 2, setlocal_2, getlocal_2, returnvalue. Another: pushbyte 3, setlocal_3, getlocal_3, returnvalue.\n\n- **else NotImplementedError**: code = b'\\xff' (255) -> raises NotImplementedError('Unsupported opcode 255'). Covers 829-831.\n\n- Lines 833-834: after the while loop ends via return, `avm_class.method_pyfunctions[func_name] = resfunc; return resfunc`. Every successful extract_function call covers these. Also caching: second call to extract_function with same name returns cached resfunc (line 440-441). Actually test 1 covers patched; the caching path (442-443) is covered by calling extract_function twice.\n\nWait, line 442-443: `if func_name in avm_class.method_pyfunctions: return avm_class.method_pyfunctions[func_name]`. After first extract_function call, resfunc is stored in method_pyfunctions. So a second call covers 442-443. I'll add that in a test.\n\nNow, about `undefined` and `_Undefined`: `pushundefined` pushes `undefined` \u2014 wait, where does `undefined` come from in resfunc? Looking at line 499: `stack.append(undefined)`. `undefined` is a module-level global in swfinterp.py. Yes: `undefined = _Undefined()`.\n\nNow about `StringClass` \u2014 need to check it's module-level. Let me check via get_info.\n\nAlso `_builtin_classes` \u2014 module-level dict.\n\nNow, about the multinames: `self.multinames` is a list where entries can be strings or _Multiname instances. In real parsing, multinames come from the ABC. For our tests, we set `interp.multinames = ['length', 'split', ..., _Multiname(...)]` and reference by index.\n\nLet me now check the actual `_Multiname` class:\n\n```python\nclass _Multiname(object):\n    def __init__(self, kind_name):\n        self.kind = kind_name\n        self.name = None\n        self.ns = None\n        self.index = None\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return '_Multiname(%r)' % (self.name,)\n```\n\nSomething like that. get_info will tell.\n\nNow, `_ScopeDict.__getitem__` falls back to `self.avm_class.constants[k]` \u2014 raises KeyError if not there.\n\nLet me also double check `extract_function`'s use of `avm_class.methods` \u2014 does `_AVMClass.__init__` create `self.methods = {}`? Let me check. In the parsing code:\n\n```python\navm_class = self._classes_by_name[cname] = _AVMClass(...)\n...\navm_class.methods[method_name] = method\n```\n\nHmm, and in `extract_function` line 446: `if func_name not in avm_class.methods`. So `methods` must exist. Let me check `_AVMClass.__init__` via get_info.\n\nActually, I recall the real code:\n\n```python\nclass _AVMClass(object):\n    def __init__(self, name, resolved_name, class_body=None):\n        self.name = name\n        self.resolved_name = resolved_name\n        self.class_body = class_body\n        self.method_names = {}\n        self.method_pyfunctions = {}\n        self.static_properties = {}\n\n        self.variables = _ScopeDict(self)\n        self.constants = {}\n        self.methods = {}\n```\n\nHmm, not sure about `methods`. Let me check.\n\nAlso, is there a `_AVMClass_Method`? Hmm. In swfinterp.py, methods are instances of... Let me think. The parsing code:\n\n```python\nfor (m_id, m_name, m_kind, m_flags, m_reg_count, m_code) in method_signatures:\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, I really need to look. Let me use get_info on `SWFInterpreter` and related.\n\nActually, get_info returns \"information about a symbol\" \u2014 probably its source code. Let me query.\n\nPlan of get_info calls (batch 1):\n- SWFInterpreter\n- _AVMClass\n- _AVMClass_Object\n- _ScopeDict\n- _Multiname\n- _Undefined\n- undefined\n- _builtin_classes\n- StringClass\n- _read_byte\n- _u30\n- _s24\n\nThat's 12 calls. All independent, so batch them.\n\nThen based on results, write the test file.\n\nLet me think about what the test file needs:\n\n```python\nimport math\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.swfinterp import (\n    SWFInterpreter, _AVMClass, _AVMClass_Object, _ScopeDict, _Multiname,\n    undefined, _builtin_classes, StringClass,\n)\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import ExtractorError\n```\n\nHelper to build interpreter:\n\n```python\ndef make_interp(constant_strings=(), multinames=()):\n    interp = SWFInterpreter.__new__(SWFInterpreter)\n    interp._classes_by_name = {}\n    interp.constant_strings = list(constant_strings)\n    interp.multinames = list(multinames)\n    interp._patched_functions = {}\n    return interp\n```\n\nWait \u2014 does SWFInterpreter have other attributes used by extract_function? Lines 439-834 use: self._patched_functions, self._classes_by_name, self.constant_strings, self.multinames. That's it. Good.\n\nHelper to build avm class with method:\n\n```python\nclass _FakeMethod(object):\n    def __init__(self, code, local_count=0):\n        self.code = code\n        self.local_count = local_count\n\n\ndef make_avm_class(name='(test)'):\n    avm_class = _AVMClass(name, name.strip('()'))\n    return avm_class\n```\n\nHmm, _AVMClass(name, resolved_name). Let me just use _AVMClass('(test)', 'test').\n\nIf `methods` isn't in __init__, I'll set `avm_class.methods = {}` manually. get_info will clarify.\n\nEncoding helpers:\n\n```python\ndef u30(v):\n    assert v >= 0\n    out = bytearray()\n    while True:\n        b = v & 0x7F\n        v >>= 7\n        if v:\n            out.append(b | 0x80)\n        else:\n            out.append(b)\n            return bytes(out)\n\n\ndef s24(v):\n    if v < 0:\n        v += 1 << 24\n    return bytes((v & 0xFF, (v >> 8) & 0xFF, (v >> 16) & 0xFF))\n```\n\nBut I must match the decoder. If `_u30` is implemented as reading variable-length 7-bit groups, my encoder matches. If `_s24` reads 3 bytes little-endian with sign bit at 0x800000, my encoder matches. get_info will confirm.\n\nRunner:\n\n```python\ndef run(interp, avm_class, code, local_count=0, args=(), func_name=None):\n    if func_name is None:\n        func_name = 'f%d' % next(_counter)\n    avm_class.methods[func_name] = _FakeMethod(code, local_count)\n    func = interp.extract_function(avm_class, func_name)\n    return func(list(args))\n```\n\nUse itertools.count for unique names.\n\nNow write the tests. Let me enumerate them concretely with byte sequences. I'll define mnemonic constants for readability:\n\n```python\nOP_LABEL = 9\nOP_JUMP = 16\nOP_IFTRUE = 17\nOP_IFFALSE = 18\nOP_IFEQ = 19\nOP_IFNE = 20\nOP_IFLT = 21\nOP_PUSHNULL = 32\nOP_PUSHUNDEFINED = 33\nOP_PUSHBYTE = 36\nOP_PUSHSHORT = 37\nOP_PUSHTRUE = 38\nOP_PUSHFALSE = 39\nOP_PUSHNAN = 40\nOP_DUP = 42\nOP_PUSHSTRING = 44\nOP_PUSHSCOPE = 48\nOP_CONSTRUCT = 66\nOP_CALLPROPERTY = 70\nOP_RETURNVOID = 71\nOP_RETURNVALUE = 72\nOP_CONSTRUCTSUPER = 73\nOP_CONSTRUCTPROPERTY = 74\nOP_CALLPROPVOID = 79\nOP_NEWARRAY = 86\nOP_FINDPROPSTRICT = 93\nOP_FINDPROPERTY = 94\nOP_GETLEX = 96\nOP_SETPROPERTY = 97\nOP_GETLOCAL = 98\nOP_SETLOCAL = 99\nOP_GETPROPERTY = 102\nOP_INITPROPERTY = 104\nOP_CONVERT_I = 115\nOP_COERCE = 128\nOP_COERCE_A = 130\nOP_COERCE_S = 133\nOP_DECREMENT = 147\nOP_TYPEOF = 149\nOP_ADD = 160\nOP_SUBTRACT = 161\nOP_MULTIPLY = 162\nOP_MODULO = 164\nOP_BITAND = 168\nOP_EQUALS = 171\nOP_GREATEREQUALS = 175\nOP_INCREMENT_I = 192\nOP_GETLOCAL0 = 208\nOP_GETLOCAL1 = 209\nOP_GETLOCAL2 = 210\nOP_GETLOCAL3 = 211\nOP_SETLOCAL0 = 212\nOP_SETLOCAL1 = 213\nOP_SETLOCAL2 = 214\nOP_SETLOCAL3 = 215\n```\n\nNow the multinames list. I'll build a fixed list:\n\n```python\nMULTINAMES = [\n    'length',      # 0\n    'split',       # 1\n    'charCodeAt',  # 2\n    'slice',       # 3\n    'join',        # 4\n    'String',      # 5\n    'reverse',     # 6\n    'notImplemented',  # 7\n    'bar',         # 8\n    'baz',         # 9\n    'm1',          # 10\n    'k',           # 11\n    'voidm',       # 12\n    'm2',          # 13\n    'sp',          # 14\n    'zz',          # 15\n    'ip',       